      [
        "Define the behavioral contract",
        "Overload selection is compile-time and each signature needs coherent semantics. Write that rule in terms a caller or operator can observe. The central failure to prevent is ambiguous overloads or implicit conversions select surprising behavior. A useful design states inputs, outputs, ownership, state transitions, and failure behavior before choosing syntax.",
        "The practical response is to keep overload families small and document units and null policy. This keeps the decision attached to the boundary that owns it. Convenience abstractions do not erase latency, authority, mutation, or lifecycle costs, so those properties remain part of the review."
      ],
      [
        "Test the boundary and retain uncertainty",
        "Collect evidence through compile-time call-site tests and runtime result assertions. A good check can fail when the mechanism is removed, identifies the artifact and setup, and distinguishes rejection, absence, delay, and success rather than collapsing them into one green result.",
        "The conclusion remains limited: a method name alone does not define its contract. Use the fixture to trace one declared invariant, then add integration, concurrency, browser, runtime, or operational evidence wherever the actual boundary requires it. Human educational review remains pending."
      ]
